{
  "dom_id": "step-q86308",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/ab5fb36385ed57a83a8bef13b0e58745fef3454d4e3fae0c54491d80b61637ce.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=3$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/7eb820e9dedb816318045df9f8dbef2d4b3007446599e56414a803f277acd622.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,3)$ and increasing $↗$ on $(3,\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left and the right of $x=3.$\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/7dd5014441f5cbe3ba3ee0127feb9cea1f8d264255e1c907ce6d5fdc458aa77f.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/fb709866c18a2ecd61160330bd54c44b481a311e3a1bec589ef26ab8a4c6243f.png)\n\n**Note:** The graph we obtained is not the only possible one. Any similar graph that's shifted vertically up or down could also be considered a possible $y=f(x).$",
